Generate All Subsequences (Basic Recursion/Backtracking)

Given an array of integers or strings generate and print all possible subsequences.
A subsequence is a sequence derived from the array by deleting some or no elements without 
changing the order of the remaining elements.

Input: arr = [3, 1, 2]
Output: [], [3], [1], [2], [3,1], [3,2], [1,2], [3,1,2]

void printSubsequences(int ind, vector<int>& ds, vector<int>& arr, int n) {
    //Base Case: When we reach the end of the array 
    if (ind == n) {
        if (ds.empty()) {
            cout << "{}" << endl;
        } else {
            for (auto it : ds) {
                cout << it << " ";
            }
            cout << endl;
        }
        return;
    }

    //Pattern: Take the current element 
    ds.push_back(arr[ind]);
    printSubsequences(ind + 1, ds, arr, n);

    //Backtrack to remove the element before moving to Not Take 
    ds.pop_back();

    //Pattern: Do not take the current element 
    printSubsequences(ind + 1, ds, arr, n);
}

int main() {
    vector<int> arr = {3,1,2};
    vector<int> ds;
    printSubsequences(0, ds, arr, arr.size());
    return 0;
}

TC: O(2^n * n)
There are 2^n possible subsequences and printing/copying each takes O(n) time.

SC: O(n)
For the recursion stack space and the auxiliary data (ds) holding at most n elements.

1. Using a std::string (For String Subsequences)
If your input is a string instead of an array, you use a string to store the current sequence. 
Strings are much easier to print because you dont need a for loop.

void printSubsequences(int ind, string& ds, string& str, int n) {
    if (ind == n) {
        if (ds.empty()) cout << "{}" << endl;
        else cout << ds << endl; //Direct printing
        return;
    }

    //Take 
    ds.push_back(str[ind]);
    printSubsequences(ind + 1, ds, str, n);

    //Backtrack
    ds.pop_back()l

    //Not Take 
    printSubsequences(ind + 1, ds, str, n);
}

2. Using a std::stack (No Iterators)
A stack follows LIFO (Last In, First Out). 
The primary challenge is that stacks do not support for (auto it : ds). 
To print it, you either have to pop elements off a copy of the stack, or use a workaround.

void printSubsequences(int ind, stack<int>& ds, vector<int>& arr, int n) {
    if (ind == n) {
        if (ds.empty()) {
            cout << "{}" << endl;
        }else {
            //Must make a copy coz popping to print destroys the stack 
            stack<int> temp = ds;
            while (!temp.empty()) {
                cout << temp.top() << " ";
                temp.pop();
            }
            cout << endl;
        }
        return;
    }

    //Take 
    ds.push(arr[ind]); 
    printSubsequences(ind + 1, ds, arr, n);

    //Backtrack
    ds.pop(); 

    //Not Take 
    printSubsequences(ind + 1, ds, arr, n);
}